WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.150
- When it opened in 1981,
the Humber Bridge

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:07.120
was the longest single-span
suspension bridge in the world.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.100
More than 1,400 metres of road,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.970
weighing 17,000 tonnes, held up by cables

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.210
that weigh thousands
of tonnes themselves.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:20.320
Those cables are made up
of more than 14,000 individual wires,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.520
all anchored into concrete
foundations on the shore.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.260
And those cables, and the
whole structure in turn,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.660
is held up by these enormous towers.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.090
The bridge is so wide and so tall that,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.360
while both towers are vertical,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.180
they're just a couple of centimetres
further apart at the top

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.620
because of the
curvature of the earth.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.490
Nearly 40 years after it opened,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.740
this bridge is about a third of the way
through its design lifespan,

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.370
and it's time for a checkup.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.450
- Back in the '90s, in the United States,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.280
bridge engineers started to unearth

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.390
severe cable corrosion in
their early structures.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.530
So, they were building
suspension bridges in the '30s.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.460
The way that the cables were
designed to be kept watertight

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.810
was, once the wires had been spun

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.810
and formed a cable and compacted,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.630
the open wires were
coated with lead paste,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.240
which is lead and linseed oil.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.940
Wrapping wire was then added

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.570
around the outside of the cable,
trapping the lead paste

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.800
and effectively keeping
the cable watertight,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.750
and it was thought that
would be sufficient.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.420
Inspections in the United States

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.150
demonstrated that moisture
was still penetrating

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.420
and gathering in the main cables

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.110
and causing significant corrosion,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:44.110
because, inside here was its
own mini climate, in effect,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.360
so it was affected by
external temperatures,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.922
day, night, winter, summer.

00:01:49.922 --> 00:01:52.120
- This is a brutal
environment for the cables.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.560
We're near the ocean, for a start,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.290
and down by the road there's
engine exhaust and spray.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.420
Remember, the cables aren't
one sold piece of metal.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.300
They're made up of thousands
of individual wires.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.740
And while they're packed tightly,

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.950
they're cylinders, so there will always be
a slight air gap between them,

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.490
and that air gap is the solution.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.020
- Water-based corrosion of steel or iron

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.220
cannot occur below 40% relative humidity.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.270
There's approximately 15-20% open space

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.830
in this compacted cable.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.220
If you can keep that volume below 40%,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.470
then effectively corrosion is halted.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.730
Inside the main deck of the bridge,

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.000
there are plant rooms that
produce dehumidified air.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.970
That air is then pumped
up to the main cable

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.310
and injected into a stainless steel sleeve

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.930
that surrounds the main cable.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.500
The relative humidity of the
air blown into the cables

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.810
is low enough to have an
affinity to collect moisture

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.860
as it travels through the cable,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.640
drying the cable and exhausting
the moisture to atmosphere,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:05.880
and that is a continuous,
24-hour cycle, 365 days a year,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.820
essentially for the rest
of the bridge's life.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:14.020
So, the Humber Bridge Board
first inspected their main cables in 2009,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:18.950
and then deployed dehumidification
the following year, 2010.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.780
Here we are, 10 years later in 2019,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.150
reinspecting the main cable
to ascertain the effectiveness

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.980
of that dehumidification system.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.470
What we do is, with a sledge hammer,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:35.300
we drive the wedges in to form
an open wedge in the cable.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.330
From each wedge line,
the consultant engineer

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:42.730
will select a wire, and then
our guys will come along

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.920
and sample that particular wire

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.350
and remove approximately
a five-meter length

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:53.760
to then take the data that they need
to collect from the cable.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.170
We then select a five-meter length
from the drum of the new wire

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.080
and splice that in with ferrules
and then remove the wedges,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.980
and that wedge line is then finished.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.300
That is then repeated seven more times
around the circumference of the cable,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.560
and then that effectively
is the inspection complete.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.400
With 100-ton hydraulic
jacks in each corner,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.400
we compress the cable back
to its original diameter.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.830
- The idea that the wires
are slightly corroded

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.890
could be worrying, but it's well, well
within design tolerances,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.770
and it's been noticed
early and dealt with,

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.030
and they're keeping a
watch on them just in case.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.840
But bridges elsewhere in the
world that aren't inspected,

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.160
where infrastructure isn't
maintained, where cutbacks

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.863
mean that this isn't happening?

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:43.130
Those should give you just
a little cause for concern.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.363
The Humber Bridge is doing just fine.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.406
That works?
All right.

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:50.890
We're going to go for the drone shot.

